<h2>What makes a game Like Outer Wilds?</h2>
<p>
    Outer Wilds successfully captures the feeling of grand discovery by:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Making the player curious</li>
    <li>Letting them explore on their own</li>
    <li>Rewarding them with cool, unexpected ideas</li>
    <li>Giving the ideas meaning</li>
</ul>
<p>
    I've named these four aspects <em>Mystery</em>, <em>Agency</em>, <em>Twists</em>, and <em>Worldbuilding</em>, respectively.
    When I review a game, in addition to my own personal score,
    {%- call sidenote("sn-metroidbrainia") %}
    My score for a game has nothing to do with whether a game is an Epiphany Game. I like other kinds of games too!
    {% endcall %}
    I also score it on each of these four aspects.
</p>

<h3>Twists</h3>
<p>
    An Epiphany Game should trigger epiphanies. It should include big, surprising secrets that completely change how the player sees the game.
</p>
<p>
    It takes a lot of creativity to come up with something genuinely surprising.
    There can be no formula for making good twists, because in order to properly explode the player's mind,
    twists need to be novel ideas the player hasn't thought of.
    Twists are therefore subjective - they depend on the player's experience.
    When I first played Antichamber, the non-Euclidean map was a surprising twist.
    But when I played <a href="/glow/lingo/">Lingo</a> years later, the same mechanic no longer felt as impressive
    (as evident by the fact that I'm not even spoiler-tagging it).
</p>
<p>
    A game scores highly on Twists if it blew my mind, and if its secrets meaningfully change the game and not just the story.
</p>

<h3>Mystery</h3>
<p>
    An Epiphany Game should make the player <em>want</em> to discover its secrets.
    It should tease incomplete or disconnected information so that the player, not the game, is asking the questions.
    That way, they are invested in the answers.
</p>
<p>
    Secrets only become mysteries when there are clues. Running into random walls to find a hidden door is not a good mystery.
    A map that must be deciphered to find that same hidden door, in contrast, is a mystery.
</p>
<p>
    A game scores highly on Mystery if it continually evokes curiosity as part of its core gameplay.
    If I'm still up playing the game at 4am because I <em>have</em> to know the answers, it's probably a high-Mystery game.
</p>

<h3>Agency</h3>
<p>
    There are lots of books and movies out there that contain mysteries and twists. 
    The difference between <em>Planet of the Apes</em> and an Epiphany Game is player agency.
    Interactivity and control over the experience is a major reason why people would choose to play a video game rather than watch a movie.
    Epiphany Games should let the player choose their own path, so that the accomplishment of discovery feels earned.
</p>
<p>
    In a pre-programmed game, it is impossible to create a truly open-ended experience.
    But limitless freedom isn't necessarily the goal - players may feel lost in a game with too little guidance.
    The goal is to make the player <em>feel</em> like they are in control, like their decisions affect the game, even while nudging them in certain directions.
    Some of my favorite Epiphany Game moments have come from trying to "break" the game, only to find that I was meant to break the game all along.
</p>
<p>
    A game scores highly on Agency if it lets the player choose the path to discovery, and every player's journey is unique.
</p>

<h3>Worldbuilding</h3>
<p>
    A good Epiphany Game should take place in a wondrous world.
    Whether or not it has characters or even physical locations, the game should provide the player with a coherent universe to become immersed in.
    Epiphanies are made so much more meaningful when the player feels some attachment to the world.
</p>
<p>
    There is some overlap with the Mystery axis here. 
    Part of the reason worldbuilding is important is that an interesting world makes the player want to explore the game.
    But its importance goes beyond its effect on player curiosity.
    Worldbuilding gives reason to otherwise arbitrary game mechanics.
    For example, let's say there is a secret ability where you can pull out a big ol' sword by pressing "S" seven times.
    This feels random and bad on its own, but it can be made less arbitrary if there is an in-universe reason.
    Perhaps you are a lonely robot wandering a post-apocalyptic world,
    and among some ruins you have found schematics of your own design that include a hidden big ol' sword.
</p>
<p>
    Good worldbuilding should be logically consistent, so as not to take the player out of their immersion.
    The pieces should all fit together as a cohesive universe, and important questions should have pleasing, satisfactory answers.
    <a href="/glow/outerwilds/">Outer Wilds</a> and Subnautica do this well - by the end, everything makes sense.
</p>
<p>
    A game scores highly on Worldbuilding if the setting is interesting, meaningful, and believable on its own terms.
    If I find myself wanting to read other players' theories on the game's universe and characters after I finish playing, it's a sign of strong Worldbuilding.
</p>

